#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111616} :activity/id #uuid "81ff0889-c17a-56c6-8185-25db1c62a977" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050126 :topic/id #uuid "2ecca3ac-5287-5fee-a69c-92dc6af52b32" :topic/math-academy-id 1532} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111907 :tutorial/content "To plot the graph of a logarithmic function, it helps to remember that the inverse of a logarithmic function is an exponential function. So, we can just plot the exponential function and reflect it over the line $y=x$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2 x\n$$\n\nThe corresponding inverse function is $y=2^x$, and since $y=2^x$ is a one-to-one function defined for all real $x$, we can obtain the graph of $y=\\log_2{x}$ by reflecting $y=2^x$ over the line $y=x$. The result is shown below:\n\n![](images/0c/0c614201798324262429f080d8e8a31bfefe58c4008bfe82e6da1b91dc158b7a.png)\n\nHere are some key points to note about\n\n$$\ny=\\log_2(x)\n$$\n\n- The domain is $x>0$, and the range is $-\\infty < y < \\infty$.\n- The graph has the vertical asymptote $x=0$.\n- The function is an increasing function.\n- The graph passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n- For $x>1$, the graph is positive and grows very slowly as we increase $x$. We say that $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ (slowly) as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n\nIn general, for any base $n>1$, the graph of $y=\\log_n{x}$ always has the same shape, and all of the above points are valid. We can verify that this is true by inspecting some possible graphs shown below:\n\n![](images/91/91567f282acbc3b8390da1e4147b445018ae649d913205e59867d58ea980ff7b.png)\n\n**Note:** The important points covered above also apply to two familiar logarithms.\n\n- $y = \\ln x = \\log_e x$ is the natural logarithm. Since the base is $n=e>1$, all of the important points covered above apply.\n- $y = \\log x = \\log_{10} x$ is the logarithm with base $10$. Since the base is $n=10>1$, all the important points covered above apply." :tutorial/id #uuid "018709be-13e5-5b9c-9eea-914f4766db68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6826 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f215c216-0d9e-543c-8c84-565a34c57a7a" :step/math-academy-id 41439 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110676 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110302 :question/id #uuid "cb79d4e2-53ee-5a09-93ad-0b2e1bdc645e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10925"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6914109-37d4-5b87-a64d-f919a124377d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050469}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111384}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing Logarithmic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f8b649fd-84bb-56cc-a44a-03501f998f2e" :step/math-academy-id 41440 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110303 :question/id #uuid "428c177d-8b83-50ad-9840-0dd3ffdf14ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-10926"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5468f23-98bd-52d9-ba21-85e5eea058a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234082}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Domain and Range of a Logarithmic Function"} :step/id #uuid "21fd8b86-49ec-5eeb-8347-f310bd707102" :step/math-academy-id 41443 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110304 :question/id #u